ROS 2 Autonomous Mobile Robot Architecture
1. Node List and Responsibilities
Core Navigation Nodes
o	map_server: Loads and serves the static map from file to other nodes
o	amcl: Performs particle filter-based localization using laser scans and odometry
o	planner_server: Implements global path planning algorithms (A*, Dijkstra, etc.)
o	controller_server: Executes local path following with velocity control
o	behavior_server: Manages high-level decision making and behavior switching
State Management
o	state_machine: Central logic coordinator that manages robot states (idle, navigating, obstacle avoidance, etc.)
o	goal_checker: Validates and processes incoming goal poses
Sensor Integration
o	laser_scan_processor: Preprocesses raw laser scan data for navigation
o	odometry_publisher: Integrates wheel encoders to provide robot pose estimates
Visualization & Monitoring
o	rviz: Real-time visualization of map, paths, sensors, and robot status
o	robot_state_publisher: Publishes robot's TF tree for coordinate transformations
Communication Bridge
o	bridge_node: Handles communication between different ROS versions if needed
2. Key Topics, Message Types, and Node Connections
Topic	Message Type	Publisher	Subscriber(s)
/scan	sensor_msgs/LaserScan	laser_scan_processor	amcl, planner_server, controller_server
/odom	nav_msgs/Odometry	odometry_publisher	amcl, planner_server, controller_server
/map	nav_msgs/OccupancyGrid	map_server	amcl, planner_server
/cmd_vel	geometry_msgs/Twist	controller_server	motion_controller
/goal_pose	geometry_msgs/PoseStamped	user_interface	state_machine, goal_checker
/path	nav_msgs/Path	planner_server	rviz, controller_server
/initialpose	geometry_msgs/PoseWithCovarianceStamped	rviz, teleop	amcl
/tf	tf2_msgs/TFMessage	robot_state_publisher, amcl	all nodes requiring coordinate transforms
3. Data Flow Explanation
Input Processing Chain
1.	Sensor Data Collection:
o	Laser scanner continuously publishes /scan topic at ~10Hz
o	Wheel encoders integrate to provide /odom pose information
o	Static map loaded by map_server and published on /map
2.	Localization:
o	AMCL subscribes to /scan, /odom, and /map
o	Uses particle filtering to determine robot's position within the map
o	Publishes /tf transforms and updates robot state
3.	Path Planning:
o	Planner receives goal via /goal_pose and current position from /odom
o	Performs global path planning through obstacle-free waypoints
o	Publishes /path topic for visualization and local control
4.	Motion Control:
o	Controller receives /path and current /odom pose
o	Implements local navigation algorithms like Dynamic Window Approach (DWA)
o	Calculates appropriate linear and angular velocities in /cmd_vel
5.	Execution:
o	Motion controller node converts /cmd_vel commands to actual motor signals
o	Feedback loop continuously adjusts based on real-time sensor data
Complete Flow Sequence
[User] → /goal_pose → [State Machine] → [Planner Server] → /path
                     ↘                        ↘
                     [Goal Checker]            [Map Server]
                                               ↗
[Sensor Data] → [Laser/IMU/Odom] → [AMCL] → [Pose Estimation]
                                               ↘
                                        [Controller Server] → /cmd_vel
                                               ↗
[Odometry + Map] ← [Path Calculation]
This architecture provides robust navigation capabilities with clear separation of concerns, enabling scalable component replacement and maintenance.

